/**
@page model Mathematical model and numerical methods

@section model_cosserat The Cosserat kinematic model

The library implements the torsionally-compliant kinematic model of Rucker,
Jones & Webster (IEEE T-RO 2010). Each pre-curved tube @e i has bending
stiffness @f$EI_i@f$, torsional stiffness @f$GJ_i@f$ and pre-curvature
@f$u^*_i = [u^*_{x,i}, u^*_{y,i}, 0]@f$ over its curved section. When the
tubes are assembled concentrically, moment balance across the assembly gives
the resultant curvature of tube 1 (the backbone),

@f[
u_{1,xy}(s) \;=\; \frac{m_{b,xy}(s) + \sum_i R_z(\theta_i)\,K_i\,u^*_i}
                      {\sum_i EI_i},
@f]

with @f$\theta_i@f$ the twist angle of tube @e i relative to tube 1
(@f$\theta_1 \equiv 0@f$) and @f$K_i = \mathrm{diag}(EI_i, EI_i, GJ_i)@f$.
The state evolves along arc length @f$s@f$ as

@f[
\begin{aligned}
u_{z,i}' &= \frac{EI_i}{GJ_i}\left(u_{x,i}\,u^*_{y,i} - u_{y,i}\,u^*_{x,i}\right), \\
\theta_i' &= u_{z,i} - u_{z,1}, \\
m_{b,xy}' &= -\left(\hat{u}_1\, m_b\right)_{xy} - \left(\hat{e}_3 R_1^{\!\top} f\right)_{xy}, \\
r' &= R_1 e_3, \qquad h' = \tfrac12\, h \otimes [0,\, u_1],
\end{aligned}
@f]

where @f$f@f$ is an optional distal point force (global frame) and
@f$m_b = [m_{b,x},\, m_{b,y},\, \textstyle\sum_i GJ_i u_{z,i}]@f$.
Material discontinuities (a tube's curved section starting, a tube ending)
split the backbone into at most six segments with piecewise-constant
parameters (ctr::Segment).

@section model_bvp Forward kinematics: a shooting BVP

The proximal twist angles follow from the actuation
(@f$\theta_i(0) = \alpha_i - \beta_i u_{z,i}(0)@f$, re-referenced so
@f$\theta_1 \equiv 0@f$), but five proximal values are unknown:
@f$m_{b,x}(0), m_{b,y}(0)@f$ and the three torsional curvatures
@f$u_{z,i}(0)@f$. They are determined by the distal boundary conditions

@f[
m_{b,xy}(L) = \left(R_1^{\!\top} w_m\right)_{xy}, \qquad
GJ_1 u_{z,1}(L) = e_3^{\!\top} R_1^{\!\top} w_m, \qquad
u_{z,i}(L_i) = 0 \;\; (i = 2, 3),
@f]

with @f$w_m@f$ an optional distal moment. The residual is
**non-dimensionalized** (all rows in [1/m]) so the single tolerance and the
5×5 finite-difference Jacobian are well-scaled; see @ref conventions.

Root-finding strategies (selected via ctr::RootFindingMethod): a modified
Newton-Raphson with Armijo line search (default; falls back to a
zero-restarted Broyden, then dog-leg, then Levenberg-Marquardt on hard cold
starts), Levenberg-Marquardt, Powell dog-leg, and Broyden. All linear
algebra in the solvers is fixed-size and allocation-free.

**Unloaded fast path:** with no distal load the transverse proximal moment
vanishes identically, so the moment rows reduce to the trivial constraints
@f$x_0 = x_1 = 0@f$ and the problem effectively has three unknowns; the BVP
Jacobian then costs 3 integrations instead of 5.

@section model_integration Integration

The 15-dimensional state is integrated segment-by-segment with a classic
fixed-step RK4 (default step 1 mm, CTR::setIntegrationStep). At 1 mm the tip
is accurate to about @f$3\times10^{-11}@f$ m against a refined reference —
seven orders of magnitude below a typical 0.5 mm position tolerance.

Fixed-step (rather than error-controlled) integration is deliberate: the
finite-difference Jacobians depend on the truncation error cancelling
between nominal and perturbed shots, which requires a deterministic step
sequence.

@section model_ik Inverse kinematics

CTR::solveIK performs damped-least-squares iteration in task space:

1. **Exact total Jacobian.** @f$J = \partial r/\partial q -
   (\partial r/\partial x)(\partial F/\partial x)^{-1}(\partial F/\partial q)@f$
   by the implicit function theorem on the shooting residual
   @f$F(x, q) = 0@f$ (CTR::kinematicJacobian) — differentiating the tip at
   frozen shooting variables (as naive finite differencing does) yields a
   systematically wrong Jacobian.
2. **Cap-normalized metric.** β [m] and α [rad] are incommensurable, so the
   step is computed in a joint space scaled by the per-iteration caps
   (ctr::IKOptions), making the damping and the step limit fair across
   joint types.
3. **Levenberg-Marquardt step control.** Trial configurations are evaluated
   through warm-started BVP solves; a failed solve or an error increase
   rejects the step and raises the damping. The solver never continues from
   an unconverged state, and on exit the robot is re-actuated at the
   accepted configuration so object state always matches the returned
   ctr::IKResult.

The iteration is local: for targets far across the workspace, seed it
closer (or multi-start) — ctr::IKResult reports the achieved error honestly,
which makes external multi-start loops trivial to write.
*/
